{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And many evils and troubles shall find him.</b> In tractate Ḥagigah (5a): Rabbi Yohanan, when he would reach this verse (31:17) — 'And it shall be, when many evils and troubles find him' — would weep. He said: A servant whose master brings upon him many evils — is there any remedy for him? What is 'and troubles'? Rav said: That troubles are made one for the other, such as a wasp and a scorpion. And Shemuel said: This refers to one who lends money to a poor man in his time of distress — that is to say, not beforehand. Rava said: That is what people say: Small coins for a child are not found, but for a third [time] they are found. Rashi explained that Shemuel is referring to the verse that he expounded above: 'Whether it be good or whether it be evil' (Ecclesiastes 12:14), and Rabbeinu Tam challenged his words: why did he interrupt the words of Rabbi Yohanan? Rather, certainly Shemuel is referring to the words of Rabbi Yohanan, and so it also implies in the Yalkut (31:946), which cites the words of Shemuel on the verse 'And it shall be, when they find him.'" "And one may raise a question about the words of Rabbi Yohanan, who said: A servant whose master brings upon him many evils — is it the Holy One, blessed be He, who brings the evils? Is it not so that 'out of the mouth of the Most High proceeds not evil' (Lamentations 3:38)? Rather, the evil comes on its own through the hiding of His face, blessed be He, as it is written: 'And I will hide My face from them, and it shall be for devouring' — that is, of itself it will be for devouring, and this is not called 'bringing.' One may also question: behold it is already written, 'And many evils and troubles shall find him' — so why did Rabbi Yohanan not weep when reading this verse, which is written before the verse 'And it shall be, when they find him'? For since he said 'when he reached this verse,' it implies that he read the portion from the beginning. It is true that we may say he was precise about the word 'וְהָיָה' ('and it shall be'), which indicates certainty, as it is written (Deuteronomy 21:14): 'וְהָיָה אִם לֹא חָפַצְתָּ בָּהּ' — Rashi explained: the verse informs that he will ultimately come to hate her, and our master Rabbi Nathan explained that Rashi was precise about the word 'וְהָיָה,' which implies that this will surely come to pass. So too, 'וְהָיָה כִּי תִמְצֶאןָ' indicates that they will surely find him. But above, it says only 'וְהָיָה לֶאֱכֹל,' whereas with regard to 'many evils and troubles one for the other' he did not use the word 'וְהָיָה,' as though the matter is in doubt whether they will find him or not." "And the words of Shemuel are extremely astonishing: to interpret this verse about one who lends to a poor man in his time of distress, as though all the reproofs would not turn him back for the good except for this trouble — that he lends to him at his time of distress and not before — and as though this is what causes him to say: 'In that day, is it not because my Elohim is not in my midst that these evils have found me' — and may it be His will that no greater trouble than this befall Israel! How can one fit into the section of great and terrible punishments the matter of lending to a poor man at his time of distress? This is something the intellect refuses to accept altogether. And further, one must resolve a matter with which the commentators struggled: what is it that the verse says: 'And he will say in that day: Is it not because my Elohim is not in my midst that these evils have found me? And I, I will surely hide My face in that day' — now the essence of idolatry is in the heart, for everything that depends on faith has its root and source in the heart. For this reason it is said regarding idolatry (Ezekiel 14:5): 'In order to take hold of the house of Israel in their heart.' And when he says 'Is it not because my Elohim is not in my midst,' he admitted with a full mouth that all the gods of the nations are idols and denied idolatry. So what claim of justice does he still have to cry out 'the face of the King YHWH' (II Samuel 19:29)? And how did He double his punishment after the confession and say: 'And I, I will surely hide My face'? And some say that this hiding refers to His hiding His face from the evil that he has done, and 'כִּי פָנָה' is an expression of wrath — despite everything, meaning that even from this great evil HaShem will hide His face, as it is written (Psalms 66:18): 'If I regarded iniquity in my heart, HaShem would not hear.'" "And I say to explain the words of Rabbi Yohanan in light of the principle of mutual responsibility (עֲרְבוּת): that all Israel became guarantors for one another, to the point that every individual bears the sin of the many. And this is a thing the public cannot endure, therefore Rabbi Yohanan wept over it when he reached this verse: 'And it shall be, when they find him — many evils.' For the word 'אֹתוֹ' ('him') appears superfluous, for it should have said: 'And it shall be, when many evils find him,' as it says above: 'And many evils and troubles shall find him.' Rather, through the word 'אֹתוֹ' he pointed with a finger at the individual who bears upon himself the evils — that is, the transgressions of many groups, as it is written (Isaiah 53:12): 'And he bore the sin of many.' And the reason he used 'רַבּוֹת' ('many,' in the feminine form) is that every sinner drains his strength like a female, in the manner that Moses said: 'You have drained my strength like a female' — as Rashi explained in the portion of Va'etḥanan (5:24) on the verse 'And you shall speak to us.'" "And He said <b>'and troubles'</b> — because in a time when there is peace and friendship among Israel, then the mutual responsibility does not harm them so much, and on the contrary, it benefits them. For in a time when one sees his fellow deviating from the path of reason, he guides him in the way he should go, to return him to the good — out of the fear of mutual responsibility. For reproof is found only from a lover to a beloved, as it is written (Proverbs 3:12): 'For whom HaShem loves, He reproves.' And so the commandment 'You shall surely reprove your fellow' (Leviticus 19:17) is juxtaposed to the commandment 'And you shall love your fellow as yourself' (Leviticus 19:18), because reproof is found only in a time when they love one another. But in a time when there is no peace in their circles, then not only does he not reprove him, but on the contrary, he rejoices in his downfall, in order to heap evil upon him and add to his transgression. And if you say that perhaps some wholehearted man might be found who would reprove even his enemy — behold, the enemy does not accept reproof from him, for he is suspect in his eyes, as though he does not intend for his benefit. If so, mutual responsibility strikes a great blow among Israel in a time when there is no peace between them and they are like troubles one for the other, like a wasp and a scorpion. For what appears good and upright in the eyes of this one appears evil in the eyes of his fellow, and vice versa — to the point that they are never in agreement: what this one prohibits, that one permits." "Therefore Rabbi Yohanan, when he reaches this verse, ״וְהָיָה כִּי תִמְצֶאןָ אוֹתוֹ רָעוֹת רַבּוֹת״, probes the word ״אוֹתוֹ״ — which is in the singular: even a single individual bears many evils, the evils that the wicked committed, which are likened to many females. And the reason for all this is ״וְצָרוֹת״ — that every Israelite becomes, each one, a foe to his fellow like a scorpion and a wasp, and they are never in one accord, for if they were in one accord no harm would be drawn from the principle of suretyship. Therefore Rabbi Yohanan wept and said: A servant whose master brings upon him many evils and lays upon him the burden of bearing the sin of the many at a time when all are foes to one another — is there any remedy for him? Surely there is no remedy at all in this manner, as it says: ״וְרָדַף אֹתָם קוֹל עָלֶה נִדָּף,״ as we explained in parashat Bechukotai (Leviticus 26:36), which speaks of this very matter — that every Israelite is pursued by his fellow Israelite. Therefore it says immediately afterward: ״וְכָשְׁלוּ אִישׁ בְּאָחִיו״ (Leviticus 26:37), and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 27a): on account of the sin of his brother. This is what the verse says: ״וּמְצָאֻהוּ רָעוֹת רַבּוֹת וְצָרוֹת וְאָמַר בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא הֲלֹא עַל כִּי אֵין אֱלֹהַי בְּקִרְבִּי מְצָאוּנִי הָרָעוֹת הָאֵלֶּה״ — for if my Elohai were within me, in my heart and my thought, and every one would state his view for the sake of Heaven when the leaders of the nation assemble together, then they would not be divided, for the true ultimate purpose unites them. However, since his intent was not for the sake of Heaven, but rather from the outside each one would present himself as though his intent were for the sake of HaShem, in the manner of dyed hypocrites, but inside him there was no 'my Elohai' — rather each one sought his own advantage or to provoke his enemy — therefore a crooked judgment emerged, for the truth will never become clear through deliberations that are not for the sake of Heaven." "And according to this, this confession is not a confession of the sin of idolatry, for the sin remains in force in any case; rather, he admits that the intent of his innards is not toward Heaven even now, for he did not say ״עַל כִּי לֹא הָיָה אֱלֹהַי בְּקִרְבִּי״ — which would imply that he wishes to turn back from that crooked path — but rather ״עַל כִּי אֵין אֱלֹהַי בְּקִרְבִּי״ even now, and then he is worse than he had been before, for he knows his Master yet intends to rebel against Him. Therefore He doubled the concealment and said: ״עַל כָּל הָרָעָה אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה כִּי פָנָה אֶל אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים,״ and פְּנִיָּה means only in the heart, as it says: ״וְאִם יִפְנֶה לְבָבְךָ״ (Deuteronomy 30:17), meaning that from the outside he would present himself as upright, as though his intent were toward Heaven, while inside he turned toward Elohim acherim and alien intentions. And therefore they were never in one accord and the truth was never clarified among them." "But from what it says, ״וְאָנֹכִי הַסְתֵּר אַסְתִּיר פָּנַי בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא,״ it appears to me along the lines of what we explained in our work Ollalot Ephraim (part 1, chapter 3) on the statement of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Bereshit Rabbah 38:6): Even at a time when Israel worship idolatry and there is peace among them — leave them be, as it says (Hosea 4:17): ״חֲבוּר עֲצַבִּים אֶפְרַיִם הַנַּח לוֹ״; however, ״when their heart is divided — now they shall bear guilt״ (Hosea 10:2). For at a time when there is peace among them, then surely all the worshippers of that idolatry will debate among themselves, and from that debate it will become clear that the worshippers have inherited lies. And therefore ״הַנַּח לוֹ״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, prolongs His anger toward them so that from themselves they will understand that all their deeds are emptiness. But at a time when the heart of the worshippers is divided and there is no peace in their circles, even if they deliberate in the matter the truth will never be clarified among people who are debating the matter with their hearts divided one from the other, for the mind of each is to refute the words of his fellow. Therefore it says: ״עַתָּה יֶאְשָׁמוּ״ — immediately and at once, for why should He grant them time for nothing? Therefore it says also here: ״וְחָרָה אַפִּי בוֹ בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא״ — immediately on that very day when they begin to worship it, for this parashah speaks at a time when Israel are foes to one another and are not in one accord; therefore ״עַתָּה יֶאְשָׁמוּ״ without any extension of time. And likewise the confession ״עַל כִּי אֵין אֱלֹהַי בְּקִרְבִּי״ indicates, in the manner explained above, that he admits that never did he set his mind toward Heaven with mouth and heart equal, but rather ״בְּפִיו שָׁלוֹם אֶת רֵעֵהוּ יְדַבֵּר וּבְקִרְבּוֹ יָשִׂים אָרְבּוֹ״ (Jeremiah 9:7). Therefore it says: ״וְאָנֹכִי הַסְתֵּר אַסְתִּיר פָּנַי בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא״ — immediately, as it says ״עַתָּה יֶאְשָׁמוּ.״ And this is a very fitting allusion." "And Samuel said that this parashah incriminates Israel, saying that such is their habit of folly — that they never do repentance except after compulsion, for at a time when the sufferings are piled upon his shoulders, then he vows vows to give his money to charity or to return to HaShem, as explained in parashat Bechukotai (Leviticus 27:2) in the juxtaposition of ״כִּי יַפְלִיא לִנְדֹּר נֶדֶר״ to the rebuke section. For our Rabbis of blessed memory also distinguished between those who do repentance from love and those who do so from fear, as is found in tractate Yoma (86b): Resh Lakish said, Great is repentance, for deliberate sins are accounted for him as unwitting ones, as it says (Hosea 14:2): ״שׁוּבָה יִשְׂרָאֵל עַד ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ כִּי כָשַׁלְתָּ בַּעֲוֹנֶךָ״ — עָוֹן means a deliberate act, and yet it calls it מִכְשׁוֹל, does it not? But Resh Lakish said: Great is repentance, for deliberate sins are accounted for him as merits, as it says (Ezekiel 33:19): ״וּבְשׁוּב רָשָׁע מֵרִשְׁעָתוֹ״ etc. Rashi explained: as it says, ״עֲלֵיהֶם הוּא יִחְיֶה״ — upon what he did, even upon the transgressions. There is no difficulty: the one speaks of repentance from love, the other of repentance from fear. And yet the matter still requires explanation as to the meaning of the verses, for even though Rashi explained ״עַד ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ as meaning 'until there is no prosecuting angel turned into defending angel,' behold, fear on account of the attribute of Justice — that He would not turn ״ה׳״ the attribute of Mercy into ״אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ the attribute of Justice — notwithstanding, ״כִּי כָשַׁלְתָּ״ is language of transgression: it does not make sense, for behold now, through repentance, the sin has become a stumbling block." "And it appears to me that the intent of the verse is to warn him to repent from love, for everyone who fears draws away from that which he fears, whereas everyone who loves draws near to his beloved. Therefore it says: ״שׁוּבָה יִשְׂרָאֵל עַד ה׳״ — to Him literally, in the manner of a lover who cleaves to Him. And concerning this our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Pesachim 50a): It is written (Psalms 57:11): ״כִּי גָדוֹל עַד שָׁמַיִם חַסְדֶּךָ,״ and it is written (Psalms 108:5): ״כִּי גָדוֹל מֵעַל שָׁמַיִם חַסְדֶּךָ״ — the one speaks of those who act from love, etc. — meaning that the one who acts from fear does not cleave to Him, blessed be He, and his attachment reaches only עַד שָׁמַיִם, not at all; therefore the kindness too is עַד שָׁמַיִם. But the one who acts from love cleaves מֵעַל שָׁמַיִם to Him, blessed be He; therefore the kindness too is מֵעַל שָׁמַיִם. And He says: Because until now you have done repentance only from fear, this is what it says: ״כִּי כָשַׁלְתָּ בַּעֲוֹנֶךָ״ — meaning, regarding the past, when you did repentance from fear, you were not cleansed entirely but only by halves, for you had already stumbled in your sin: the deliberate sin became an unwitting stumbling block, and if so, the trace of the stumbling block remains in you and you were not cleansed entirely from the sin, for it had become an unwitting act through it, and you still require a sacrifice for the unwitting act. But when you return עַד ה׳ from love, which is ״מֵעַל שָׁמַיִם,״ then you will be cleansed entirely and you will need not even a sacrifice but only confession. This is what it says (Hosea 14:3): ״קְחוּ עִמָּכֶם דְּבָרִים וְשׁוּבוּ אֶל ה׳״ — to Him literally. ״אִמְרוּ אֵלָיו כָּל תִּשָּׂא עָוֹן״ — entirely may you forgive iniquity, not by halves as is the way of one who repents from fear, whose sin is not borne entirely but only by halves, making the deliberate into unwitting. However, when you return from love, then ״אִמְרוּ כָּל תִּשָּׂא עָוֹן״ — entirely may you forgive iniquity, so that it becomes like a commandment, a thing borne aloft and very high — not as it says (Micah 7:19): ״וְתַשְׁלִיךְ בִּמְצֻלוֹת יָם כָּל חַטֹּאתָם,״ but rather the iniquity shall be borne aloft. ״וְקַח טוֹב״ — that the Holy One, blessed be He, should take it as a good thing, as though the iniquity were a merit, a good thing; and then you will not need even a sacrifice. This is what it says: ״וּנְשַׁלְּמָה פָרִים שְׂפָתֵינוּ.״ However, in Ezekiel (33:14) it is written: ״וּבְאָמְרִי לָרָשָׁע מוֹת תָּמוּת וְשָׁב מֵחַטָּאתוֹ״ etc. ״חָיֹה יִחְיֶה,״ and it does not say ״עֲלֵיהֶם חָיוֹ יִחְיֶה,״ because this speaks of one who repents from fear, on account of the decree ״מוֹת תָּמוּת,״ which is from fear." "However, afterward it says: ״וּבְשׁוּב רָשָׁע מֵרִשְׁעָתוֹ״ — that is, not out of fear of death but ״מֵרִשְׁעָתוֹ״, of himself, for he recognizes that the way he is walking is not good; then ״עֲלֵיהֶם הוּא יִחְיֶה״, for this one repents out of love from the perspective of his wickedness alone." "And as for what is said (Ezekiel 18:27), ״וּבְשׁוּב רָשָׁע מֵרִשְׁעָתוֹ״ etc., and it is not written there ״עֲלֵיהֶם חָיוֹ יִחְיֶה״ — this is because it speaks of one who repents out of fear, for it is said there (Ezekiel 18:28): ״וַיִּרְאֶה וַיָּשָׁב״ etc., and the word ״וַיִּרְאֶה״ is an expression of seeing — that is, he reflects upon the sufferings that have come upon him. Therefore it says there immediately (Ezekiel 18:30): ״שׁוּבוּ וְהָשִׁיבוּ״ etc. ״וְלֹא יִהְיֶה לָכֶם לְמִכְשׁוֹל עָוֹן״, for it is known that one who repents out of fear seeks only to save himself, and does not seek to bring back others. But one who repents out of love also brings back others, for one who loves the king wants everyone to love Him; therefore it says: ״שׁוּבוּ וְהָשִׁיבוּ״, and then ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לָכֶם לְמִכְשׁוֹל עָוֹן״ — rather, you will be cleansed from everything, and no trace of the stumbling block will remain. It appears from this that one who repents out of fear is inferior to one who repents out of love; but one who repents after coercion, when sufferings have already come upon him, is even more inferior, for when the compelling distress ceases he reverts to his transgression. As it is said of Pharaoh (Exodus 8:11): ״וַיַּרְא כִּי הָיְתָה הָרְוָחָה וְהַכְבֵּד אֶת לִבּוֹ״, and as it is said (Exodus 10:3): ״עַד מָתַי מֵאַנְתָּ לֵעָנֹת מִפָּנָי״ — that is, not because of sufferings but ״מִפָּנָי״. For the contrition that comes through sufferings is nothing, for after the sufferings pass you return to your transgression. Thus any repentance performed under the compulsion of sufferings is nothing, for once the storm and tempest pass he reverts to his former state, as it is said (Isaiah 28:24): ״הֲכֹל הַיּוֹם יַחֲרוֹשׁ הַחוֹרֵשׁ״, and the entire parable there is said concerning this matter." "And this is what it says: ״וּמְצָאֻהוּ רָעוֹת רַבּוֹת וְצָרוֹת, וְאָמַר בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא הֲלֹא עַל כִּי אֵין אֱלֹהַי בְּקִרְבִּי מְצָאוּנִי הָרָעוֹת הָאֵלֶּה״. Behold, in these two words, ״בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא״, He sealed the entire matter, for they encompass few days — both the preceding days and the following days. For before the many evils and troubles found him, it is obvious that he would not have taken it to heart of his own accord to return to HaShem. But even after those evils found him, nevertheless he confesses only ״בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא״ — when the troubles are heaped upon his shoulders, but not on the next day, for immediately the following day, once the storm and tempest of HaShem's wrath pass, he reverts at once to his transgression. Therefore ״וְאָנֹכִי הַסְתֵּר אַסְתִּיר פָּנַי״ also ״בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא״ — even on the day he confesses, I will hide My face, and more so than when I hid My face in the past, but rather in double measure, twice over, ״הַסְתֵּר אַסְתִּיר פָּנַי״, for He sees the troubles bundled in his storehouse and even so his heart is not whole with HaShem. For HaShem knows that today he will speak with contrition, as it were stealing the mind of the One above, and tomorrow he will revert to his transgression. And this is what Samuel said: this is the one who lends to the poor in the time of his distress — and בִּשְׁעַת דָּחְקוֹ does not refer to the distress of the poor but to the distress of the lender, for this is a lender who lends to the poor nothing except בִּשְׁעַת דָּחְקוֹ — in the time of the giver's distress, when the troubles compel him to give charity from his money; and when that distress passes, his heart is stout and mighty as before. And he singled out one commandment, namely charity, for it is the way of the world that people vow in the time of their trouble and after the trouble they regret and give nothing at all — and the same holds for other commandments, which are performed only at the time when compulsion rides upon his shoulders. And by way of allusion he alludes to all the commandments of the Torah, which are like a loan and charity to a poor child — that is, the good impulse, as Hillel said, who went to perform kindness for this guest etc. (Leviticus Rabbah 34:3)." "And Rava said: this is what people say: זוּזֵי לְעַלְלָא לָא שְׁכִיחֵי לִתְלִיתָא שְׁכִיחָא. לְעַלְלָא — that is, the season of cheapness, the time when prices are cheap in the world, and this is precisely the time of tranquility, for when prices are cheap in the world, tranquility is in the world — the one depends on the other, as it is written (Psalms 147:14): ״הַשָּׂם גְּבוּלֵךְ שָׁלוֹם חֵלֶב חִטִּים יַשְׂבִּיעֵךְ״. And at that time when the עַלְלָא, that is, the produce, is plentiful in the world, at that time it is not found at all that a person gives of his money to charity — ״וַיִּשְׁמַן יְשֻׁרוּן וַיִּבְעָט״. But לִתְלִיתָא שְׁכִיחָא — whether according to Rashi's explanation that תְּלִיתָא means loss and destruction, or according to Rabenu Tam who explained לִתְלִיתָא as the fear of the ruler who terrifies — in either case it speaks of a time when troubles are prevalent in the world, namely loss and destruction, or the fear of the rulers of the nations who rule over Israel — all of it speaks of a time of compulsion, and this is not complete repentance. Also it alludes to one who is pressed in a court below regarding charity, when the court compels him to give charity — all these repentances are not complete, for they occur only at a time when compulsion is present, and when the compelling distress ceases he reverts to his transgression. Therefore it says: ״וְעַתָּה כִּתְבוּ לָכֶם אֶת הַשִּׁירָה הַזֹּאת״ — with the word ״וְעַתָּה״ He sealed the entire matter, to say: now, immediately and at once, while you are still in your tranquility, before the days of evil come — ״כִּתְבוּ לָכֶם אֶת הַשִּׁירָה הַזֹּאת״, that it be engraved upon your hearts, so that you may return to HaShem with complete repentance, not by way of compulsion." "And in my small work Orach le-Chayim we explained this entire passage in another manner: that the פָּנִים mentioned here are faces of wrath, as in ״וְשַׂמְתִּי פָּנַי בָּאִישׁ הַהוּא״ (Leviticus 20). And we said that after this complete confession HaShem will hide His face of wrath; therefore the hiding is doubled, for a good measure is doubled. ״עַל כָּל הָרָעָה״ etc. — it states a superlative: even though he committed one evil that encompasses all evils, for one who confesses to idolatry is as one who denies the entire Torah (Sifrei Shelach 15:22), therefore it says ״עַל כָּל הָרָעָה״ — even upon all the great evil that he did, ״כִּי פָנָה אֶל אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים״, nevertheless: הַסְתֵּר אַסְתִּיר — faces of anger from him, and all is permitted and forgiven after the complete confession. And regarding His saying ״וְאָמַר בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא״ etc., we explained there that this verse encompasses all the foundations of repentance. Seek His dwelling and there you will find the entire matter explicated."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {2 "In the final clause, 'וְ״כִי פָנָה״ לְשׁוֹן אַף עַל פִּי' — the phrase עַל פִּי here most plausibly means 'despite everything' (literally 'on the mouth of'), though it could conceivably bear the sense 'according to,' i.e., taking כִּי פָנָה as an idiom of wrath; the passage is texturally dense and the exact nuance uncertain.", 3 "The wordplay on רַבּוֹת as a feminine form, linked to the idea of the sinner's strength being drained 'like a female' (כַּנְקֵבָה), depends on the grammatical observation that רַבּוֹת shares its form with feminine plural morphology; the Kli Yakar appears to connect this to Rashi on Deuteronomy 5:24 where Moses rebukes the people with הִתַּשְׁתֶּם כֹּחִי כִּנְקֵבָה.", 5 "The Hebrew cites Leviticus 26:36 as ״(כו לו)״ and the subsequent Leviticus 26:37 as ״(כו לז)״ — both retained exactly as written.", 13 "The Aramaic aphorism זוּזֵי לְעַלְלָא לָא שְׁכִיחֵי לִתְלִיתָא שְׁכִיחָא (b. Bava Batra 8a) is rendered according to the Kli Yakar's own extended glosses on each term.", 14 "The Sifrei citation reference '(ספרי שלח טו כב)' appears to point to Sifrei on Numbers 15:22 (parashat Shelach); the Kli Yakar's specific section numbering may vary by edition. The Leviticus citation '(ויקרא כה)' likely intends chapter 20 (where וְשַׂמְתִּי פָּנַי appears) rather than 25; rendered as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:17:31.743733970Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}